<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 1100 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Charyapada</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="pra">চর্যাপদ</bdi> / <bdi lang="pra-Latn">Caryāpada</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>Riddling Buddhist songs from eastern India, about a thousand years old, in which scholars find the roots of Bengali, Assamese and Odia.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>22 Mahasiddhas (Siddhacharyas), among them Luipa, Sarahapa and Tantripāda</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (each song headed by the raga to which it was sung)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Vajrayana Buddhist Siddhacharyas of eastern South Asia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Late Apabhraṃśa (Abahaṭṭha dialects)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Assam, Bengal, Bihar and Odisha (eastern South Asia)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>compiled c. 1000–1200 CE; scholars' dates range from the 7th to the 14th century</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (songs of the Buddhist Siddhacharyas, compiled c. 1000–1200 CE)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charyapada">Charyapada</a> is a collection of mystical poems, songs of realisation in the tantric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a> tradition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>. It was compiled between about 1000 and 1200 CE in late Apabhraṃśa, or the Abahaṭṭha dialects, at a time when the north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrits</a> had not yet divided, or were only beginning to divide, into later languages. Scholars of Assamese, Bengali, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magahi">Magahi</a>, Maithili and Odia all find features of their languages in it, and the songs are claimed as early literature in each: Luipa was from Kamarupa, and Sarahapa is said to have come from Rani near present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>; Rahul Sankrityayan held that most of the poets were from Bihar and wrote an early Magahi, and K. P. Jayaswal took the language for an early Maithili. The dating is debated. Haraprasad Shastri placed the songs in the 10th century, Suniti Kumar Chatterji and Prabodh Chandra Bagchi between the 10th and 12th, Sukumar Sen possibly as late as the 14th, while Muhammad Shahidullah put them between the 7th and 11th and Sankrityayan between the 8th and 11th.</p><p>The songs were composed by 22 of the Mahasiddhas, or Siddhacharyas, each named at the head of his pada (verse), probably by the commentator Munidatta, who wrote a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> commentary on them; the raga to which each song was to be sung is named before it. Shastri, who rediscovered a palm-leaf <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> Royal Court Library in the early 20th century, called their riddling idiom &quot;twilight language&quot; (sandhya-bhasha), &quot;half-expressed and half-concealed&quot;, after Munidatta's commentary; Vidhushekhara Shastri later named it &quot;intentional language&quot; (sandha-bhasha). Shastri published the manuscript's 47 padas with the commentary through the Bangiya Sahitya Parishad in 1916, as part of his Hajar Bacharer Purano Bangala Bhasay Bauddhagan O <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doha">Doha</a> (&quot;Buddhist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> and Couplets&quot;), under the title Charyacharyavinishchayah. Its title page, colophon and several leaves, with padas 24, 25 and 48, are lost; the manuscript is now in the National Archives of Nepal.</p><p>The songs were also kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhist</a> canon. Prabodh Chandra Bagchi published a Tibetan translation of 50 verses, which supplies the missing padas, among them pada 25 by the poet Tantripāda, whose work had been lost, and the whole of pada 23. Tibetan tradition assigns the translation of the songs to Vairocanavajra, a 12th-century monk of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nalanda">Nalanda</a>; the Tibetan text names Shilachari as translator of the songs and Chandrakirti or Kirtichandra as translator of Munidatta's commentary, the Charyagiti-koshavrtti. Some of the ragas the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> names are extinct, and some may be other names for ragas still known today.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vajrayana">Vajrayana</a> path to realisation, written in a deliberately veiled language: everyday scenes and figures carry a hidden spiritual sense meant for the initiated, so that each song works as a riddle about the nature of mind and liberation.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Late Apabhraṃśa</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Late Apabhraṃśa</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">কাআ তরুবর পঞ্চ বি ডাল</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">kāā tarubara pañca bi ḍāla</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>The body is the finest of trees; five are its branches.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">চঞ্চল চীএ পইঠো কাল॥॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">cañcala cīe pa:iṭho kāla ||||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Into the restless mind Time has entered. ||||</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">দিট করিঅ মহাসুহ পরিমাণ</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">diṭa karia mahāsuha parimāṇa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Make it firm, and take the measure of the Great Bliss:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">লুই ভণই গুরু পুচ্ছিঅ জাণ॥ ধ্রু॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">lui bhaṇa:i guru pucchia jāṇa || dhru ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lui says, ask the guru and know. || refrain ||</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">সঅল স[মা]হিঅ কাহি করিঅই</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">saala sa[mā]hia kāhi karia:i</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>All the samādhis [?]: what are they done for?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">সুখ দুখেতেঁ নিচিত মরিআই॥ ধ্রু॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">sukha dukhetem̐ nicita mariāi || dhru ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Through pleasure and pain one surely dies. || refrain ||</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">এড়িএউ ছান্দক বান্ধ করণক পাটের আস</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">eṛieu chāndaka bāndha karaṇaka pāṭera āsa</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Cast off the bond of desire [?] and the hope in the craft [?] of the senses [?];</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">সুনুপাখ ভিতি লাহু রে পাস॥ ধ্রু॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">sunupākha bhiti lāhu re pāsa || dhru ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>take the side of the Void [?] close to you. || refrain ||</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">ভণই লুই আম্‌হে সাণে দিঠা</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">bhaṇa:i lui āmhe sāṇe diṭhā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Lui says: I have seen it in meditation [?];</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="pra">ধমণ চমণ বেণি পাণ্ডি বইণ॥ ধ্রু॥</span><span class="sg-r" lang="pra-Latn">dhamaṇa camaṇa beṇi pāṇḍi ba:iṇa || dhru ||</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>dhamaṇa and camaṇa, the two, I sat on as my seat [?]. || refrain ||</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Luipa, pada 1, ed. Haraprasad Shastri (1916). Text from <a href="https://bn.wikisource.org/wiki/%E0%A6%B9%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%9C%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B0_%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%9B%E0%A6%B0%E0%A7%87%E0%A6%B0_%E0%A6%AA%E0%A7%81%E0%A6%B0%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%A3_%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%99%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%97%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B2%E0%A6%BE_%E0%A6%AD%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%B7%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%AF%E0%A6%BC_%E0%A6%AC%E0%A7%8C%E0%A6%A6%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%A7%E0%A6%97%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%A8_%E0%A6%93_%E0%A6%A6%E0%A7%8B%E0%A6%B9%E0%A6%BE_(%E0%A7%A7%E0%A7%AF%E0%A7%A7%E0%A7%AC)%2F%E0%A6%9A%E0%A6%B0%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%AF%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%AF%E0%A6%BE%E0%A6%9A%E0%A6%B0%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%AF%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%AF-%E0%A6%AC%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%A8%E0%A6%BF%E0%A6%B6%E0%A7%8D%E0%A6%9A%E0%A6%AF%E0%A6%BC%2F%E0%A7%A7" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ISO 15919).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p><p>The original is hard to read in places, and our translation of those lines is tentative.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (13)</summary><ul><li>The language of the Charyapada (an early stage of Bengali, close to the Apabhraṃśa of the time) is old and the songs speak in a deliberately veiled 'twilight language' (sandhyābhāṣā) of Buddhist tantric practice: many words have more than one accepted reading, and editors and translators differ on several lines of this song. The translation follows the plainest sense of the words; the [?] marks show where it is least sure.</li><li>Romanization: ISO 15919 applied to the Bengali letters of Shastri's edition, as on the site's other Bengali songs: the inherent vowel written (ḍāla, kāla), ব as b, ড় as ṛ, ঁ as m̐; the colon marks a vowel letter standing after a (pa:iṭho, bhaṇa:i, karia:i, ba:iṇa), so that a + i is not read as the diphthong ai. The double dandas are given as ||; the editor's bracketed letters (স[মা]হিঅ) are kept.</li><li>|| refrain ||: ধ্রু (dhru, for dhruvapada) is the edition's mark of the refrain; the doubled double danda after line 2 (॥॥) is printed as in the source.</li><li>line 1: kāā is the body (kāya); tarubara, 'the best of trees'; 'pañca bi ḍāla', 'five (are) the branches': commonly read as the five senses or the five aggregates (skandhas) of Buddhist teaching.</li><li>line 2: cīe, 'in the mind' (citta); kāla is time and also death; pa:iṭho, 'has entered'.</li><li>line 3: diṭa, 'firm' (dṛḍha); 'what is to be made firm' is not said: usually taken as the mind. mahāsuha (mahāsukha), the Great Bliss of tantric Buddhism.</li><li>line 4: Lui (Luipa) names himself, as each Charyapada poet does.</li><li>line 5: sa[mā]hia, the edition's restoration, read as samādhi (meditative absorption) [?]; kāhi, 'why, for what'. Readers differ on whether the line asks what samādhi is for or says that all of it is useless.</li><li>line 6: nicita, 'surely' (niścita); mariāi, '(one) dies'. Often read as saying that one who clings to pleasure and pain, even through such practices, still dies.</li><li>line 7: the line is among the least certain [?]: chāndaka bāndha, 'the binding of chanda' (desire, will; also deceit or a scheme); karaṇaka pāṭera āsa, 'the hope of the karaṇa's pāṭa' (karaṇa: the senses, or ritual practices; pāṭa: craft, order, a seat). eṛieu, 'give up, cast off'.</li><li>line 8: sunu, 'the Void' (śūnya); pākha, 'side, wing' (pakṣa); bhiti is read as 'clinging, drawing near' or as 'wall'; 'lāhu re pāsa', 'take (it) to your side'. The sense of the whole line is disputed [?].</li><li>line 9: āmhe, 'we, I'; sāṇe is read as 'in meditation' (dhyāna) [?]; diṭhā, 'seen'.</li><li>line 10: dhamaṇa and camaṇa are left untranslated: they are usually explained as two channels or two breaths of the yogic body, sometimes as two contrary forces, which the yogi masters; pāṇḍi read as 'seat' [?], ba:iṇa as 'sat, seated' [?]; beṇi, 'both, the two'.</li></ul></details></section>
